{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791303060,"nonce":"yMM3do9b/66yMc8/C3EcG31bRZA=","message":"Landed 678dc991a0939a90548a95b289249cd04a13892d, after LLVM companion 856e0699b93bde3383da555c6ec6883d9beb6c7b. The shared HIR pass proves a scalar state is preserved on every reachable backedge, adds a guarded copy, supports entering after generic iterations, and merges live exits. Copies exclude allocations/releases/callbacks, have bounded size/count, and retain the generic path. Tests cover transition into a stable state, rejecting a changing state, and a shared exit with a bypass edge across interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM.\n\nValidation: normal interpreter-first tiering, LLVM backend verified for both binaries. Local compiler regressions (6 bounds, 3 loop specialization, 14 OSR transfer, 1 promoted stub) and local Lua/Python conformance (43/50 categories) passed. NUC compiler regressions all 24 passed; full Lua conformance 46/46 including official, official_complete and warm_up::official passed; full Python conformance 50/50 passed. NUC conformance logs: /tmp/zyntax-select-opt-nuc-tests.log and /tmp/zyntax-select-opt-nuc-conformance.log.\n\nAll 17 Lua kernels passed checksum validation in alternating rounds. The final 7-round repeat held /tmp/zyntax-nuc.lock and used taskset -c 0-7 equally for every runtime (NUC P cores). Collatz medians: prior 150.520 ms kernel /174.715 ms wall; select pass alone 152.348/176.178; both changes 116.100/139.543; LuaJIT 132.924/134.933. Combined kernel time falls 22.9%, wall time 20.1%; LuaJIT still wins wall time by about 4.6 ms. Mandelbrot and integer-call apparent regressions did not repeat under these controls. Results: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-select-opt-fp-lua-ab.json.\n\nThe complete Python speed suite passed all 22 supported kernel checks with the initial select-pass candidate; pidigits remains unsupported. Locked 3-round repeats of the final candidate show SOR 9112.654 -\u003e 9134.995 ms (unchanged), sparse matrix 151.563 -\u003e 149.402 ms, Monte Carlo 47.143 -\u003e 49.199 ms. This does not close the broad Python/Lua performance gaps. Results: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-select-opt-fp-speed-ab.json. Concurrent Rayzor builds made the later Mac timing probe unsuitable; it was discarded.","files":null}]}